1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,130
Most people think a valuation is a guess

2
00:00:03,130 --> 00:00:06,710
based on what similar properties
have sold for nearby.

3
00:00:06,740 --> 00:00:09,690
That's part of it, but
on its own it misses

4
00:00:09,690 --> 00:00:12,960
what actually drives the value
of an investment property.

5
00:00:12,960 --> 00:00:15,440
In this video I'm going
to show you the process

6
00:00:15,440 --> 00:00:18,520
we actually use to value
a property and why

7
00:00:18,520 --> 00:00:22,360
it's different for residential compared
to commercial and investment property.

8
00:00:22,360 --> 00:00:24,960
I'm Jake, the founder of Calibre Acquire.

9
00:00:24,960 --> 00:00:28,690
I've worked in property for 14
years and transacted on over £80

10
00:00:28,690 --> 00:00:32,070
million worth of commercial property
investments in the past year alone.

11
00:00:32,070 --> 00:00:34,800
I help investors get out of
underperforming property

12
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:37,800
assets into ones that actually
work harder for them.

13
00:00:37,800 --> 00:00:40,090
For a standard residential property, value

14
00:00:40,090 --> 00:00:42,680
mostly comes from comparable evidence.

15
00:00:42,680 --> 00:00:46,250
What have similar properties on
similar streets sold for recently?

16
00:00:46,250 --> 00:00:50,000
Size, condition and location
adjust the number from there.

17
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:53,040
For an investment or commercial
property, the rent it produces

18
00:00:53,040 --> 00:00:56,920
matters just as much as the
comparables, sometimes more.

19
00:00:56,920 --> 00:00:59,640
Two buildings can look
identical and sell for very

20
00:00:59,640 --> 00:01:02,400
different prices because the
income behind them is different.

21
00:01:02,400 --> 00:01:04,680
The first thing we look at is the income.

22
00:01:04,680 --> 00:01:07,170
What rent is the property
producing, and what

23
00:01:07,170 --> 00:01:09,800
rent could it reasonably
produce if it were let?

24
00:01:09,800 --> 00:01:11,960
This isn't just a headline rent figure.

25
00:01:11,960 --> 00:01:14,030
We look at the lease terms, who the

26
00:01:14,030 --> 00:01:17,480
tenant is and how secure
that income actually is.

27
00:01:17,480 --> 00:01:20,660
If you're considering a property
and want to stress test it within a

28
00:01:20,660 --> 00:01:26,250
few minutes, I built calculators for mixed-use,
HMO and single residential deals, so you

29
00:01:26,250 --> 00:01:29,540
can put in the real numbers and
see what a deal actually returns.

30
00:01:29,540 --> 00:01:30,400
completely free.

31
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:33,840
Comment the word send below, or
use the link below to get access.

32
00:01:33,840 --> 00:01:36,440
From the income we work
out the reflected yield.

33
00:01:36,440 --> 00:01:39,160
Yield is the relationship
between the income a

34
00:01:39,160 --> 00:01:42,880
property produces and its value,
expressed as a percentage.

35
00:01:42,880 --> 00:01:47,280
Different locations, asset types and
tenant strengths command different yields.

36
00:01:47,280 --> 00:01:50,530
A strong covenant on a long
lease in a good location

37
00:01:50,530 --> 00:01:53,700
will achieve a lower yield,
meaning a higher value, than

38
00:01:53,700 --> 00:01:57,000
a weaker tenant on a short
lease in a secondary location.

39
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:59,520
Alongside this, we work out the price

40
00:01:59,520 --> 00:02:02,680
per square foot based
on comparable evidence.

41
00:02:02,680 --> 00:02:05,910
What have similar buildings
in similar locations, with

42
00:02:05,910 --> 00:02:10,120
similar specifications, sold for
recently, expressed per square foot?

43
00:02:10,120 --> 00:02:12,670
This gives us a second figure to check

44
00:02:12,670 --> 00:02:15,960
the valuation against,
independent of the income.

45
00:02:15,960 --> 00:02:19,000
The final step is blending
the two together.

46
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:22,770
If the income-based valuation and
the comparable-based valuation

47
00:02:22,770 --> 00:02:25,560
are close, that gives
confidence in the figure.

48
00:02:25,560 --> 00:02:29,090
If they're far apart, that's
usually a sign something needs

49
00:02:29,090 --> 00:02:33,040
a closer look, either in the
income, the comparables, or both.

50
00:02:33,040 --> 00:02:36,090
If you're only looking at
what's sold nearby, you can end

51
00:02:36,090 --> 00:02:41,040
up overpaying for a property with
weak income, or underpaying for one

52
00:02:41,040 --> 00:02:44,360
with strong income that hasn't
been reflected in the price yet.

53
00:02:44,360 --> 00:02:47,110
Understanding both sides
of the valuation gives you

54
00:02:47,110 --> 00:02:50,040
a much clearer picture of
what you're actually buying.

